<body>
<div class="page">

  <h1>NATS Bonus Creator</h1>
  <p class="doc-subtitle">Mac Setup Guide &nbsp;·&nbsp; Do this once before running any scripts for the first time.</p>

  <p class="overview-label">What you'll set up</p>
  <table class="overview-table">
    <tr><td>Step 1</td><td>Download Python from python.org</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Step 2</td><td>Install the Playwright library via Terminal</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Step 3</td><td>Install the Chromium browser via Terminal</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Step 4</td><td>Download &amp; set up Google Drive for Desktop</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Step 5</td><td>Turn on Path Bar in Finder</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>⏱ Time required</strong>
    About 15–20 minutes total. You only need to do this once — after setup, running scripts takes just seconds.
  </div>

  <hr />

  <!-- ── STEP 1 ── -->
  <h2>Step 1 — Download &amp; Install Python</h2>

  <p>Python is the programming language the automation scripts are written in. Your Mac may already have an older version, but we need <strong>Python 3.9 or newer</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Download</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">Open Google Chrome and go to <strong>python.org/downloads</strong>.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Click the big yellow <strong>Download Python 3.x.x</strong> button at the top of the page. Always use the latest version.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">The file will download to your Downloads folder. It'll be named something like <code class="inline">python-3.xx.x-macos11.pkg</code>.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Install</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">Open your Downloads folder and double-click the <code class="inline">.pkg</code> file.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Follow the installer: click <strong>Continue → Continue → Agree → Install</strong>. You may be asked for your Mac password — this is normal.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">When you see <em>"The installation was successful"</em>, click <strong>Close</strong>.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>✓ How to check it worked</strong>
    Open Terminal (explained in Step 2), type <code class="inline">python3 --version</code>, and press Return. You should see something like <code class="inline">Python 3.12.4</code>. Any version 3.9 or higher is fine.
  </div>

  <hr />

  <!-- ── STEP 2 ── -->
  <h2>Step 2 — Install Playwright in Terminal</h2>

  <p>Playwright is the library that lets Python control a web browser automatically. You install it by running one command in Terminal.</p>

  <h3>How to open Terminal</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">Press <strong>Command (⌘) + Space</strong> to open Spotlight Search.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Type <strong>Terminal</strong> and press Return.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">A window opens with a blinking cursor and a line ending in a <code class="inline">$</code> symbol. Terminal is ready.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Run the install command</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">Click anywhere inside the Terminal window to make sure it's selected.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Copy and paste the command below into Terminal, then press <strong>Return</strong>.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="cmd-block">
    <code>pip3 install playwright --break-system-packages</code>
    <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCmd(this, 'pip3 install playwright --break-system-packages')">Copy</button>
  </div>

  <ol class="steps" style="counter-reset: step 2;">
    <li><div class="step-content">Terminal will print several lines as it downloads and installs. Wait until you see the <code class="inline">$</code> prompt on a new line — that means it's finished.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">A line starting with <strong>WARNING</strong> is fine to ignore. A line starting with <strong>ERROR</strong> means something went wrong — take a screenshot for reference.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>💡 Tip</strong>
    Make Terminal text bigger with <strong>Command (⌘) + Plus (+)</strong> if it's hard to read.
  </div>

  <hr />

  <!-- ── STEP 3 ── -->
  <h2>Step 3 — Install Chromium</h2>

  <p>The scripts open a browser window to fill in forms automatically. They use Chromium (similar to Chrome) as that browser. This installs it in one command.</p>

  <h3>Run the install command</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">Your Terminal window should still be open. If you closed it, re-open it the same way as Step 2.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Copy and paste the command below, then press <strong>Return</strong>.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="cmd-block">
    <code>playwright install chromium</code>
    <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCmd(this, 'playwright install chromium')">Copy</button>
  </div>

  <ol class="steps" style="counter-reset: step 2;">
    <li><div class="step-content">Chromium is a larger download — it may take 1–2 minutes. Wait until the <code class="inline">$</code> prompt reappears.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>✓ What success looks like</strong>
    Terminal prints something like <em>"Chromium 123.x downloaded to..."</em> and then your <code class="inline">$</code> prompt reappears with no error messages.
  </div>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>⚠ Steps 2 and 3 only need to be run once.</strong>
    Python, Playwright, and Chromium are now permanently installed. You won't need to run these Terminal commands again, even after restarting your Mac.
  </div>

  <hr />

  <!-- ── STEP 4 ── -->
  <h2>Step 4 — Download &amp; Set Up Google Drive for Desktop</h2>

  <p>Scripts for RTC Top Up and Free Spins load images from a shared Google Drive folder. For this to work, Google Drive needs to be installed as a desktop app — this makes your Drive files appear as a regular folder in Finder, just like Downloads or Documents.</p>

  <p>If you already use Google Drive for Desktop and can see Google Drive in your Finder sidebar, skip to <strong>"Find the folder path"</strong> below.</p>

  <h3>Download &amp; install</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">Go to <strong>google.com/drive/download</strong> and click <strong>Download Drive for desktop</strong>.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Open Downloads and double-click the file named <code class="inline">GoogleDrive.dmg</code>.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Drag the Google Drive icon into your <strong>Applications</strong> folder.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Open Applications and double-click <strong>Google Drive</strong> to launch it.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Sign in with your Fanatics email (<code class="inline">yourname@betfanatics.com</code>) and follow the setup prompts. A Drive icon will appear in your menu bar (top-right of your screen) when it's ready.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Find the shared folder in Finder</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">Open Finder (the blue smiley face icon in your Dock).</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">In the left sidebar, click <strong>Google Drive</strong> to expand it. If you don't see it: <strong>Finder menu → Preferences → Sidebar</strong> → check the box next to Google Drive.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Navigate to:<br />
      <div class="path-example">Shared drives → Marketing Automations → Lifecycle Automations → RTC Top Up</div>
      For Free Spins, use <strong>RTC Top Up - Free Spins</strong> instead.
    </div></li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Find the folder path and enter it in the tool</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">In Finder, <strong>right-click</strong> the <strong>RTC Top Up</strong> folder and select <strong>Get Info</strong>.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">In the window that opens, find the line labelled <strong>Where:</strong>. This is the full path. It will look like:<br />
      <div class="path-example">/Users/yourname/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-yourname@betfanatics.com/Shared drives/Marketing Automations/Lifecycle Automations</div>
    </div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Click and drag to select the full path, then press <strong>Command (⌘) + C</strong> to copy it.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Open the NATS Bonus Creator in your browser, click <strong>Edit Images</strong>, and paste the path into the text field.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Add <code class="inline">/RTC Top Up</code> to the end of the path so it points to the folder itself, not its parent.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Click <strong>Save Path</strong>. This saves to your browser — you won't need to enter it again.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>💡 Example of a complete path</strong>
    <div class="path-example">/Users/john.smith/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-john.smith@betfanatics.com/Shared drives/Marketing Automations/Lifecycle Automations/RTC Top Up</div>
  </div>

  <hr />

  <!-- ── STEP 5 ── -->
  <h2>Step 5 — Turn On Path Bar in Finder</h2>

  <p>The Path Bar is a strip at the bottom of every Finder window that shows the full folder path of wherever you're browsing. Turning it on makes it much faster to copy folder paths without using Get Info every time.</p>

  <h3>Enable the Path Bar</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">Open Finder.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">In the top menu bar at the very top of your screen, click <strong>View</strong>.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Click <strong>Show Path Bar</strong>. A strip will appear at the bottom of the Finder window showing the current folder path.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Copy a path from the Path Bar</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">Navigate to the folder you want in Finder.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Right-click the folder name in the Path Bar at the bottom of the window.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Select <strong>Copy "Folder Name" as Pathname</strong>. The full path is now in your clipboard.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>💡 Tip</strong>
    This is the fastest way to grab paths for the Edit Images field — much quicker than Get Info each time.
  </div>

  <hr />

  <!-- ── RUNNING A SCRIPT ── -->
  <h2>Running a Script — Quick Reference</h2>

  <p>Once setup is complete, here's the full process each time you want to run a script:</p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div class="step-content">Open the NATS Bonus Creator (<code class="inline">nats_bonus_creator_v2.4.html</code>) in your browser by double-clicking it.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Configure your dates, amounts, and offer type, then click <strong>Generate Script</strong>. The script downloads automatically to your Downloads folder.</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Open Terminal (Command + Space → type "Terminal" → press Return).</div></li>
    <li><div class="step-content">Navigate to your Downloads folder:</div></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="cmd-block">
    <code>cd ~/Downloads</code>
    <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCmd(this, 'cd ~/Downloads')">Copy</button>
  </div>

  <ol class="steps" style="counter-reset: step 4;">
    <li><div class="step-content">Run the script. The exact filename is shown in the run card at the top right of the tool — click Copy next to it. It'll look like:</div></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="cmd-block">
    <code>python3 rtc_top_up_052626_1430.py</code>
    <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCmd(this, 'python3 rtc_top_up_052626_1430.py')">Copy</button>
  </div>

  <ol class="steps" style="counter-reset: step 5;">
    <li><div class="step-content">A browser window will open. Log in to the trading platform, then come back to Terminal and press <strong>Return</strong> when prompted. The script runs through all codes automatically and sends a notification when done.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>⚠ Important</strong>
    Do not close Terminal or the browser window while the script is running. If something goes wrong, press <strong>Control + C</strong> in Terminal to stop it safely.
  </div>

  <p class="footer-note">Questions? Contact Adrian Van de Camp on Slack.</p>

</div>

<script>
  function copyCmd(btn, text) {
    navigator.clipboard.writeText(text).then(() => {
      btn.textContent = 'Copied!';
      btn.classList.add('copied');
      setTimeout(() => {
        btn.textContent = 'Copy';
        btn.classList.remove('copied');
      }, 2000);
    });
  }
</script>
</body>
